{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"PDF generation in Django views is made possible by the open-source ReportLab Python PDF library.","source_span":"This is made possible by the excellent, open-source ReportLab_ Python PDF library."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"Django was used at kusports.com to generate customized, printer-friendly NCAA tournament brackets as PDF files for people participating in a March Madness contest.","source_span":"Django was used at kusports.com_ to generate customized, printer-friendly NCAA tournament brackets, as PDF files, for people participating in a March Madness contest."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"ReportLab can be installed with pip using the command 'python -m pip install reportlab'.","source_span":"$ python -m pip install reportlab"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"The key to generating PDFs dynamically with Django is that the ReportLab API acts on file-like objects, and Django's FileResponse accepts file-like objects.","source_span":"The key to generating PDFs dynamically with Django is that the ReportLab API acts on file-like objects, and Django's :class:`~django.http.FileResponse` objects accept file-like objects."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"The response automatically sets the MIME type application/pdf based on the filename extension.","source_span":"The response will automatically set the MIME type :mimetype:`application/pdf` based on the filename extension."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"When as_attachment=True is passed to FileResponse, it sets the Content-Disposition header, telling browsers to show a save/handle dialog.","source_span":"When ``as_attachment=True`` is passed to ``FileResponse``, it sets the appropriate ``Content-Disposition`` header and that tells web browsers to pop-up a dialog box prompting/confirming how to handle the document"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"It is important to call showPage() and save() on the PDF object to finish generating the file.","source_span":"Finally, it's important to call ``showPage()`` and ``save()`` on the PDF file."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"ReportLab is not thread-safe, which can cause odd issues in PDF-generating views accessed by many people simultaneously.","source_span":"ReportLab is not thread-safe. Some of our users have reported odd issues with building PDF-generating Django views that are accessed by many people at the same time."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","statement":"Django Packages provides a comparison of packages that help generate PDF files from Django.","source_span":"Django Packages provides a `comparison of packages <https://djangopackages.org/grids/g/pdf/>`_ that help generate PDF files from Django."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What Python library does Django use to generate PDF files dynamically?","answer":"The open-source ReportLab Python PDF library."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What real-world example is given of Django being used to generate PDFs?","answer":"Django was used at kusports.com to generate customized, printer-friendly NCAA tournament brackets as PDF files for people participating in a March Madness contest."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What command installs ReportLab using pip?","answer":"python -m pip install reportlab"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Why can Django's FileResponse be used together with the ReportLab API?","answer":"Because the ReportLab API acts on file-like objects, and Django's FileResponse objects accept file-like objects."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"What does setting as_attachment=True on a FileResponse do when serving a generated PDF?","answer":"It sets the Content-Disposition header, which tells web browsers to pop up a dialog prompting how to handle/save the document, in addition to the response's MIME type being set to application/pdf based on the filename extension."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Is ReportLab thread-safe?","answer":"No, ReportLab is not thread-safe, which has caused odd issues for views accessed by many people at once."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_outputting-pdf","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What two methods must be called on the ReportLab canvas object to finish generating a PDF?","answer":"showPage() and save()."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"Template overrides can be placed either in the project's templates directory or in an application's templates directory.","source_span":"You can either put template overrides in your project's templates directory or in an application's templates directory."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"If both an app's and the project's templates directories contain overrides, Django's default template loader tries the project-level directory first, since DIRS is searched before APP_DIRS.","source_span":"the default Django template loader will try to load the template from the project-level directory first. In other words, :setting:`DIRS <TEMPLATES-DIRS>` is searched before :setting:`APP_DIRS <TEMPLATES-APP_DIRS>`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"In the example, the third-party application 'blog' provides the templates blog/post.html and blog/list.html.","source_span":"Let's say you're trying to override the templates for a third-party application called ``blog``, which provides the templates ``blog/post.html`` and ``blog/list.html``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"To override from the project's templates directory, TEMPLATES DIRS is set to BASE_DIR / \"templates\" and override files are placed at templates/blog/list.html and templates/blog/post.html.","source_span":"\"DIRS\": [BASE_DIR / \"templates\"], \"APP_DIRS\": True"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"To override templates from an app's own directory (e.g. myapp), the overrides go in myapp/templates/blog/list.html and myapp/templates/blog/post.html, and requires APP_DIRS set to True.","source_span":"myapp/\n        templates/\n            blog/\n                list.html\n                post.html"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"You can extend a template with {% extends %} while simultaneously overriding it, for example adding a custom logo to admin/base_site.html by overriding the branding block and calling block.super.","source_span":"you can extend a template using the :ttag:`{% extends %}<extends>` template tag whilst at the same time overriding it... add a custom logo to the ``admin/base_site.html`` template"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","statement":"This extend-and-override technique works because the template loader does not consider the already loaded override template when resolving the extends tag.","source_span":"This technique works because the template loader does not consider the already loaded override template (at ``templates/admin/base_site.html``) when resolving the ``extends`` tag."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Where can you place template overrides in a Django project?","answer":"Either in the project's own templates directory or in an application's templates directory."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","fact_refs":[1],"question":"If a template of the same name exists in both an app's templates directory and the project's templates directory, which one does Django's default loader use?","answer":"The project-level directory's version, because DIRS is searched before APP_DIRS."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In the overriding-templates example, what two templates does the third-party 'blog' app provide?","answer":"blog/post.html and blog/list.html."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What DIRS setting is used to override templates from the project's templates directory in the example?","answer":"DIRS is set to [BASE_DIR / \"templates\"], with the override files placed at templates/blog/list.html and templates/blog/post.html."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How can you add a custom logo to Django's admin site while keeping the rest of the default admin template?","answer":"Create templates/admin/base_site.html containing {% extends \"admin/base_site.html\" %}, override the branding block to add the logo image, and call {{ block.super }} to retain the original branding content."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_overriding-templates","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why doesn't extending an overridden template cause infinite recursion in Django?","answer":"Because the template loader does not consider the already-loaded override template when resolving the extends tag, so it loads the original template instead of looping back to the override."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"Deploying static files to production involves two basic steps: run collectstatic when static files change, then move the collected STATIC_ROOT directory to the static file server to be served.","source_span":"The basic outline of putting static files into production consists of two steps: run the :djadmin:`collectstatic` command when static files change, then arrange for the collected static files directory (:setting:`STATIC_ROOT`) to be moved to the static file server and served."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"When serving static files from the same server as the site, the process is: push code, run collectstatic on the server into STATIC_ROOT, then configure the web server to serve STATIC_ROOT under the STATIC_URL.","source_span":"Push your code up to the deployment server. On the server, run :djadmin:`collectstatic` to copy all the static files into :setting:`STATIC_ROOT`. Configure your web server to serve the files in :setting:`STATIC_ROOT` under the URL :setting:`STATIC_URL`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"Common choices for a dedicated static file server are Nginx or a stripped-down version of Apache.","source_span":"Some common choices are:\n\n* Nginx_\n* A stripped-down version of Apache_"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"rsync is a common tool for pushing the local STATIC_ROOT to a dedicated static file server because it only transfers the bits of static files that have changed.","source_span":"`rsync <https://rsync.samba.org/>`_ is a common choice for this step since it only needs to transfer the bits of static files that have changed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"Amazon S3 is given as an example cloud storage provider for serving static files, often used together with a CDN.","source_span":"Another common tactic is to serve static files from a cloud storage provider like Amazon's S3 and/or a CDN (content delivery network)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"A custom S3 storage backend can be set for collectstatic via STORAGES = {\"staticfiles\": {\"BACKEND\": \"myproject.storage.S3Storage\"}}.","source_span":"STORAGES = {\n        # ...\n        \"staticfiles\": {\"BACKEND\": \"myproject.storage.S3Storage\"}\n    }"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","statement":"Django Packages provides an overview of third-party storage backends at djangopackages.org.","source_span":"A good starting point is the `overview at djangopackages.org <https://djangopackages.org/grids/g/storage-backends/>`_."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What are the two basic steps for putting Django static files into production?","answer":"Run the collectstatic command whenever static files change, then move the collected STATIC_ROOT directory to the static file server so it can be served."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[1],"question":"When serving static files from the same server that runs the Django site, what three steps make up the deployment process?","answer":"Push the code to the deployment server, run collectstatic on the server to copy files into STATIC_ROOT, then configure the web server to serve the STATIC_ROOT files under the STATIC_URL."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What two example web servers are mentioned as common choices for serving static files from a dedicated server?","answer":"Nginx and a stripped-down version of Apache."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Why is rsync a common choice for pushing static files to a dedicated static file server?","answer":"Because rsync only needs to transfer the parts of static files that have changed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What cloud storage provider is given as an example for serving static files, often combined with a CDN?","answer":"Amazon S3."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How would you configure Django's STORAGES setting to use a custom S3 storage backend located at myproject.storage.S3Storage?","answer":"Set STORAGES = {\"staticfiles\": {\"BACKEND\": \"myproject.storage.S3Storage\"}}."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_deployment","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Where can you find an overview of third-party storage backends for Django static files?","answer":"At the storage-backends grid overview on djangopackages.org."}
